caregiver side : A dashboard that just says ✅ or ❌ for today. That's it. She doesn't need to see the medicine names or doses necessarily. Just: did he take it or not.
She is effectively the patient's proxy — she marks doses as taken, she adds new medicines
Needs reminders: "Dad hasn't marked his 8pm medicine yet" to caregiver 
streak of meds and friendly zomato/duolingo types notifications
   16. The primary patient may not be the primary app user.
Meena (caregiver-spouse) may be the one using the app on behalf of Ramesh. Design for this. The "patient" account should allow a caregiver to operate it fully if the patient consents.
Voice output isn't a nice-to-have. It's core for Type 1 patients.
Ramesh cannot squint at a timeline. He needs the app to tell him: "Ramesh ji, abhi aapki Metformin lene ka samay hai." Web Speech API. 30 minutes to build. Do it.
The caregiver's primary question is binary: did they take it or not.
Priya doesn't want a medical dashboard. She wants a green tick or a red cross. Don't over-engineer the caregiver view.
 19. Consent isn't a compliance feature — it's emotionally real.
Ramesh might be embarrassed about a psychiatric medication. His daughter doesn't need to see that. The toggle isn't a legal checkbox — it's respecting his dignity. Frame it that way in your pitch.
 "Learned people" — no. Assume minimum literacy for the patient side.
Your UI language should be: large text, simple words, visual icons alongside text, Hindi/Marathi at minimum as a chatbot option. The caregiver (Priya, Rohan) is probably more tech-comfortable and can handle a richer dashboard.
The chatbot's real user is Meena and Priya, not Ramesh.
Ramesh will use voice output. Meena might type: "what does this medicine do?" Priya might ask: "he missed two doses, is that dangerous?" Design chatbot prompts around these actual questions.
